<!--
  <summary>
  On-screen keyboard (CGUIDialogKeyboardGeneric).
  </summary>
  <remarks>
  CRITICAL: the character keys MUST use Kodi's hardcoded grid IDs or the
  engine can't fill their glyphs or route clicks. The grid is 4 rows × up
  to 13 columns, IDs offset by 100 with rows stepping by 20:
      row 0: 100-112
      row 1: 120-132
      row 2: 140-152
      row 3: 160-172
  Kodi assigns each key's character from the active keyboard layout, the
  skin must NOT set labels on these (leave empty). Special keys keep their
  own labels; Kodi handles them by ID:
      8  = backspace      32  = space
     300 = done/OK       301 = cancel       308 = clear
     302 = shift         303 = caps lock    304 = symbols
     305 = cursor left   306 = cursor right
     311 = heading        312 = edit field

  Earlier versions gave every letter id=32 (the spacebar), so on-screen
  clicks did nothing and only a physical keyboard could type. This version
  uses the correct IDs, so both work.

  Navigation: each row is its own grouplist, positioned absolutely.
  Left/right inside a row is the grouplist's own job. Up/down is wired
  key by key in the four character rows, so Down from "t" lands on "g"
  and not on the first key of the next row: a move to a grouplist id
  goes to that row's first or last used key and loses the column. A
  grouplist keeps a child's own <onup>/<ondown> (DialogNumeric relies on
  the same thing), and a key Kodi has hidden for the current layout
  passes the move on in the same direction, so short rows need no
  special cases. That is why the character keys are written out in full
  here and do not use the KeyboardKey include, which has no navigation
  parameters. The rows keep their own <onup>/<ondown> (ids 9501 to 9506)
  as the fallback the special keys still use.

  Column map from the bottom character row to the special keys, by where
  each 80 px key sits over them (columns 0 to 12): 0 and 1 Shift, 2 Caps,
  3 and 4 Sym, 5 to 7 Space, 8 and 9 cursor left, 10 cursor right, 11
  and 12 Backspace.

  Two earlier attempts failed, in opposite ways, and both are worth keeping
  in mind:

    - Sibling grouplists with NO explicit chain. They do not link vertically
      on their own, so only the mouse worked.
    - All rows nested inside one outer vertical grouplist, to let Kodi
      auto-navigate between them. Nested groups inside a grouplist break
      focus traversal, the same trap as the MusicOSD transport row and the
      favourites category chips, so focus reached the
      top row and stopped there, leaving Done and Cancel unreachable by
      remote.

  Explicit chains between siblings is what works. Don't nest these.
  </remarks>
-->
